INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers") VALUES (460091351101019, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving engineering curves and their practical classification, such as selecting a curve construction for a cam profile or motion diagram. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving constructing an ellipse by standard geometric methods, such as drawing an ellipse when the major and minor axes are known. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving parabola and hyperbola construction and the focus/directrix idea, such as constructing a conic using a given focus and directrix. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving cycloidal curves, involutes and spirals, such as representing a point on a rolling circle or an unwinding string. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nEngineering curves include conics and other geometric curves used to represent shapes or motion. Ellipses, parabolas and hyperbolas are conic sections; cycloids, epicycloids, hypocycloids, involutes and spirals are other common constructions in engineering graphics. The method is chosen according to the defining geometry and the intended use. A drawing should show construction lines clearly, use suitable instruments and identify the required tangent or normal when requested. Accuracy depends on the construction procedure and neat, unambiguous line work.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn ellipse can be constructed by methods such as concentric circles, rectangle or oblong methods, and arc-of-circle approaches taught in the syllabus. First mark the major and minor axes and their centres accurately. Divide the relevant circles, sides or arcs into corresponding parts, project matching points and draw a smooth continuous curve through them. Select the method according to the data and construction requested. Check symmetry about both axes and use light construction lines before darkening the final curve.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA conic is defined by a geometric relation between a point and a focus/directrix or by another specified construction condition. A parabola has eccentricity one; an ellipse has eccentricity below one and a hyperbola above one. Construction methods use the given dimensions or geometric properties to locate successive points, then join them smoothly. Tangents and normals are drawn according to the prescribed method at the required point. Verify the defining dimensions and symmetry and distinguish the conics by their geometry rather than by appearance alone.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA cycloid is traced by a point on a circle rolling along a straight line. An epicycloid is generated when a circle rolls outside another circle, while a hypocycloid is generated when it rolls inside. An involute can be understood as the path traced by the end of a taut string unwound from a base curve; spirals move progressively inward or outward according to their construction. Start from the generating geometry, divide the motion into corresponding steps, plot points carefully and construct any requested tangent or normal.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

